The figure is a regular pentagon. When the sides of a regular pentagon are extended, it forms a star. The star is made up of five isosceles triangles, each with two sides equal to the length of the pentagon's side and the base equal to the length of the line segment that connects two non-adjacent vertices of the pentagon. The angle at the center of the pentagon is 72 degrees (360 degrees / 5). The angle we are trying to find, angle A, is one of the base angles of one of these isosceles triangles. Since the sum of the angles in a triangle is 180 degrees, we can find angle A by subtracting the vertex angle from 180 degrees and then dividing by 2. The vertex angle is the angle at the center of the pentagon, which is 72 degrees. Therefore, the angle at one of the non-adjacent vertices of the pentagon, which is angle A, is (180 - 72) / 2 = 54 degrees. The answer is 54.



